{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-11-marked-price-markup-target-margin",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Marked Price, Markup, and Target Selling Price",
    "description": "Set a marked price from cost and a target margin, or recover cost from a marked or final price.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Distinguish CP, marked/list price (MP), and SP. Find a required markup above CP, determine CP from a marked/final price and known margin, and work backward from a target profit after a later discount or commission. State the base for every percentage and use factors to show whether it applies to CP or MP. Check that a positive discount lowers SP below MP and that the resulting margin is measured against CP. Use solved examples 32–35 and objective patterns."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Marked Price, Markup, and Target Selling Price: Set a marked price from cost and a target margin, or recover cost from a marked or final price. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cost price (CP) is the seller’s outlay. Marked price (MP) is the displayed or list amount. Selling price (SP) is the amount actually charged after any reduction. Do not use these labels interchangeably.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A markup of m% above CP means MP=(1+m/100)CP. Markup percent is measured on CP, unless a problem explicitly calls the increase something else.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: CP ₹800, markup 50% gives MP=1.5×800=₹1,200. If the item sells at MP, profit is ₹400 or 50% of CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A marked price above CP does not guarantee profit of the same percent if a discount is offered. A 20% discount from MP ₹1,200 gives SP ₹960, only 20% profit on CP ₹800.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To reach a target profit after discount, work backward. If CP is ₹100 and the target receipt is ₹120 after a 20% discount, MP must satisfy 0.8MP=120, so MP=₹150.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A target MP can be expressed relative to CP. In that example MP ₹150 is 50% above cost, though the final SP gives 20% profit after discount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If final SP and discount are given, recover MP by dividing by the retained fraction: 15% discount means SP=85% of MP; MP=SP/0.85.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If MP and target gain are given, compute SP needed for that gain, then compare it with MP to find the maximum discount the seller can afford.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When an article is sold at a discount yet still makes a stated profit, first calculate SP from MP and discount. Then recover CP from SP and profit factor.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check the price order for a positive discount: MP≥SP. If target SP exceeds MP, the condition requires a surcharge or increase, not a discount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common mistake subtracts the discount percent from markup percent. A 50% markup followed by a 20% discount gives factor 1.5×0.8=1.2, or 20% profit—not 30%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If price has several percentage changes, use multipliers in the stated order. All factors apply to the current amount at that stage, not always to the original CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A stated sale price “including tax” may not equal the seller’s receipt if tax is collected for the government. Use only the seller’s own receipt as SP when the problem separates the tax.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Write the bases beside rates: markup on CP, discount on MP, profit on CP. This simple annotation prevents the most common cross-base errors.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Final check: apply your proposed markup and discount forward to recover SP, then compare it with CP to confirm the target profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
